const articleData=[
["Understanding Soil Health and Its Importance","Soil Management","Explore soil fertility, organic matter, soil structure and living organisms—and learn why healthy soil is the foundation of productive farms.","Healthy soil is more than a medium that holds plant roots. It is a living system made up of minerals, organic matter, water, air and countless organisms. Its physical, chemical and biological condition influences how well crops establish, access nutrients and withstand difficult seasons.","Soil structure creates spaces for air and water while allowing roots to grow. Organic matter helps bind soil particles, improves water-holding capacity and supplies energy for soil organisms. Earthworms, fungi and bacteria contribute to nutrient cycling and the breakdown of residues. Soil testing, careful traffic management, crop residues and suitable rotations can help maintain these functions.","Observe soil across the field rather than relying on one small spot. Look at drainage, compaction, surface cover and crop performance, and use a representative soil test when possible. Corrective actions should be based on test results and local advice; adding inputs without understanding the constraint can waste resources.","Fertile soil is a long-term farm asset. Protecting its structure and biological life supports more reliable crop production."],
["Modern Irrigation Techniques for Ethiopian Farmers","Irrigation and Water","Compare drip, sprinkler and surface irrigation, and consider how to match water delivery to crops, soils and available resources.","Irrigation can help farmers manage rainfall variability and support crop production when water is available. The best system depends on the water source, field shape, soil, crop, energy access, investment and maintenance capacity. No single method is suitable for every farm.","Drip irrigation delivers water close to plant roots through emitters and can reduce wetting of unused areas. Sprinklers distribute water over the crop and may suit different field layouts, but wind and evaporation can affect performance. Surface methods use gravity and may be familiar and affordable, although uneven fields and poor scheduling can lead to losses.","Check the water source throughout the season, keep filters and channels clear, and irrigate according to crop need and soil moisture rather than a fixed habit. Compare the full cost of equipment, repairs and energy before investing. Local extension officers can help assess whether a system is appropriate.","Efficient irrigation combines suitable equipment with good scheduling, maintenance and responsible water use."],
["Understanding Crop Rotation and Its Benefits","Crop Production","Learn how changing crop families across seasons can support soil fertility, break pest cycles and make better use of farm resources.","Crop rotation means growing different crops on the same field in a planned sequence over several seasons. It differs from repeatedly planting the same crop, which can encourage certain pests, diseases and nutrient imbalances. A useful rotation is designed around local rainfall, markets, soil and household needs.","Crops differ in their nutrient requirements, rooting patterns and residues. Including legumes can contribute biologically fixed nitrogen when the right crop and conditions are present. Different crop families can interrupt some pest and disease cycles, while varied roots and residues can improve soil cover and structure.","Plan a sequence rather than choosing each crop in isolation. Record what was planted in each plot, avoid following a crop with a closely related host when disease risk is high, and consider seed availability and market demand. Rotation benefits are strongest when combined with good soil cover and other sound practices.","A planned rotation spreads risk and helps maintain productive fields over time."],
["Identifying and Managing Common Crop Pests","Pest and Disease Management","Understand early scouting, pest identification and integrated pest management before deciding whether intervention is needed.","Crop pests can reduce plant growth and harvest quality, but not every insect found in a field is harmful. Some insects pollinate crops or feed on pests. Accurate identification and regular observation help farmers choose a response that is effective and avoids unnecessary expense.","Inspect fields regularly, including the underside of leaves, growing points and areas where damage first appears. Record the crop stage, affected area and pattern of symptoms. Compare what you see with trusted local identification guides, since nutrient stress, disease and weather damage can resemble insect injury.","Integrated pest management combines prevention, monitoring, cultural practices, biological controls and carefully selected pesticides when justified. Follow product labels and local regulations, use protective equipment, protect pollinators and avoid spraying when beneficial insects are active. Seek local expert advice when identification is uncertain.","The first step in pest management is correct identification—not automatic spraying."],
["The Role of Agricultural Technology in Modern Farming","Agricultural Technology","See how digital tools, sensors, mobile services and farm records can help farmers make more informed decisions.","Agricultural technology includes both simple and advanced tools: improved hand equipment, weather information, mobile advisory services, mechanization, sensors and digital record systems. Technology is useful when it solves a real farm problem and fits the people who will use and maintain it.","Digital tools can help organize planting dates, input use, field observations and market information. Weather services may support decisions about field work, while sensors can provide information about soil moisture or protected-crop conditions. Tools do not replace farmer experience; they add information that should be checked against local conditions.","Start with one practical need, such as keeping farm records or receiving weather updates. Consider connectivity, language, training, repair access, data privacy and total cost. Test a tool on a small scale before relying on it for important decisions.","The most valuable technology is accessible, understandable and useful in the farmer’s daily work."],
["Climate-Smart Agriculture and Food Security","Climate-Smart Agriculture","Explore practices that support productivity, adaptation to climate risks and responsible management of natural resources.","Climate-smart agriculture is an approach that seeks to improve agricultural productivity and incomes, strengthen resilience to climate change, and reduce or remove greenhouse-gas emissions where possible. The balance between these goals depends on local conditions and priorities.","Practices may include diversified cropping, improved soil cover, water harvesting, agroforestry, adjusted planting calendars and climate information services. These approaches can help manage risk, but their results depend on rainfall, soils, crop choice, labour and access to resources.","Farmers can begin by identifying the risks that affect their fields most: dry spells, intense rain, heat, erosion or new pest pressure. Choose measures that address those risks, combine practices where possible and review results each season. Community planning and reliable local climate information are also important.","Resilience grows through locally suitable practices, informed planning and learning from each season."],
["Organic Fertilizers and Their Benefits","Soil Management","Learn about compost, manure and crop residues, and how organic materials contribute to soil condition and nutrient cycling.","Organic fertilizers and soil amendments come from plant, animal and other biological materials. Compost and properly managed manure can add organic matter and nutrients, while crop residues protect the surface and feed soil organisms as they decompose.","The nutrient content of organic materials varies with their source, storage and treatment. Mature compost is generally more stable than fresh waste. Proper handling and composting can reduce weed seeds and some health risks. Organic inputs release nutrients at different rates, so they may not meet every crop need on their own.","Use clean materials, compost them appropriately and apply them according to crop needs and local recommendations. Avoid placing fresh manure directly against edible plant parts, and protect waterways from runoff. Where possible, combine organic matter management with soil testing and balanced nutrient planning.","Organic materials improve the soil system, but safe handling and balanced nutrient management remain essential."],
["Understanding Plant Diseases and Their Prevention","Pest and Disease Management","Recognize common disease groups, monitor symptoms and reduce the conditions that allow diseases to spread.","Plant diseases may be caused by fungi, bacteria, viruses and other organisms. Symptoms can include spots, wilting, rotting, mosaic patterns or abnormal growth. Similar symptoms can also result from nutrient problems, water stress or chemical injury, so diagnosis matters.","Fungal diseases often spread through spores and may be favoured by leaf wetness and humid conditions. Bacterial diseases can move through water, tools or infected planting material. Viruses are often spread by infected seed or planting material and insect vectors. The exact pattern depends on the crop and pathogen.","Use healthy seed and planting material, clean tools, manage crop residues, provide suitable spacing and avoid moving contaminated soil between fields. Remove or manage infected plants according to local guidance. If symptoms are unfamiliar or spreading quickly, consult an agricultural expert or diagnostic service before choosing a treatment.","Prevention, clean planting material and early diagnosis are central to responsible disease management."],
["The Importance of Quality Seeds in Crop Production","Crop Production","Understand seed selection, germination, varietal suitability and storage practices that support strong crop establishment.","Seed quality influences crop establishment and the potential of a harvest. Good seed is true to the variety, viable, physically clean and free from important seed-borne pests and diseases. Even high-quality seed needs suitable planting conditions and careful management.","Choose varieties suited to local rainfall, altitude, growing period, soil and market preferences. Check labels, source and certification where available. Germination testing on a small sample can provide a useful indication of viability, although it does not replace formal quality testing.","Keep seed dry, cool and protected from pests. Use clean containers, label each lot and avoid mixing varieties. Follow recommended planting depth and spacing, and do not keep seed from a hybrid crop for replanting unless the variety is intended for that purpose.","Good seed is an important starting point, but successful crops also depend on suitable conditions and management."],
["Sustainable Water Management in Agriculture","Irrigation and Water","Discover water conservation, rainwater harvesting, efficient field practices and ways to protect local water resources.","Agriculture depends on reliable water, yet farms share rivers, groundwater and catchments with households, livestock and ecosystems. Sustainable water management considers both farm productivity and the long-term condition of the resource.","Rainwater harvesting can capture runoff for later use when systems are safely designed and maintained. Mulching and soil organic matter can help reduce evaporation from the soil surface. Maintaining channels, repairing leaks and choosing irrigation schedules carefully can reduce avoidable losses.","Assess the source and seasonal availability of water before expanding irrigation. Keep storage areas safe, prevent contamination and follow local water-use rules. Community-level planning is important where many users depend on the same source.","Saving water on the farm also helps protect the wider landscape and the people who depend on it."],
["How Artificial Intelligence Is Transforming Agriculture","Agricultural Technology","Explore how AI can support crop monitoring, forecasting and planning—and why human judgement and reliable data still matter.","Artificial intelligence can help process large amounts of information, identify patterns and present recommendations. In agriculture, applications include image-based crop monitoring, weather and yield modelling, advisory chat systems and tools that help organize farm records.","AI systems may analyse images for possible pest or disease symptoms, combine weather data with crop information, or help summarize market trends. Their outputs are estimates, not guarantees. Poor-quality data, limited local training examples and changing field conditions can lead to incorrect suggestions.","Use AI as a decision-support tool rather than a final authority. Confirm important diagnoses with qualified local advice, check the source and date of data, and protect personal and farm information. Test recommendations on a small scale before applying them widely.","AI is most useful when its recommendations are transparent, locally relevant and checked by people with agricultural knowledge."],
["Understanding Greenhouse Farming","Agricultural Technology","Learn the basics of protected cultivation, including structures, ventilation, temperature, humidity and crop selection.","Greenhouses and other protected structures create a more controlled environment for growing crops. They can protect plants from some weather conditions and support production of high-value vegetables and seedlings, but they require investment, management and reliable access to inputs.","Temperature, humidity, light and ventilation interact. Poor ventilation can lead to excessive heat and humidity, increasing stress and disease risk. Crop selection should consider local markets, water quality, structure design and the farmer’s ability to manage the environment.","Start with a structure suited to the local climate and budget. Plan ventilation, drainage, water supply and access before construction. Monitor conditions regularly, keep the structure clean and maintain a crop calendar. Compare expected costs and market opportunities before investing.","Protected cultivation can extend production opportunities when environmental control and market planning are handled well."],
["Preventing Soil Erosion on Agricultural Land","Soil Management","Understand how water and wind remove valuable topsoil, and learn conservation practices for sloping and exposed fields.","Soil erosion removes the fertile surface layer that supports crop growth. It can be accelerated by intense rainfall, bare soil, steep slopes, poor drainage and loss of vegetation. Once topsoil is lost, restoring field productivity can take considerable time.","Contour farming, terraces, grass strips, cover crops and agroforestry can slow runoff and help hold soil in place. Residue cover protects the surface from raindrop impact, while roots help bind soil. Measures should be designed for the slope, soil and local rainfall pattern.","Keep soil covered where practical, guide runoff safely and maintain conservation structures. Avoid concentrating water into channels that may cut gullies. Work with local land and water experts on larger structures, since poorly designed terraces can create new risks.","Preventing erosion is usually more effective than trying to rebuild soil after it has been washed away."],
["Post-Harvest Management and Food Loss Reduction","Sustainable Agriculture","Follow good harvesting, sorting, handling, storage and transport practices to protect food quality and farmer income.","Post-harvest management includes all activities between harvest and consumption or processing. Losses can occur through poor timing, rough handling, pests, moisture, unsuitable storage and delays in transport. Reducing these losses can improve the amount and quality of food reaching markets.","Harvest at an appropriate maturity stage and handle produce gently. Sort damaged items, use clean containers and keep produce out of direct sun when suitable. Different crops need different temperature, humidity and ventilation conditions, so storage advice should be crop-specific.","Clean and dry storage areas before use, monitor for pests and moisture, and keep new harvests separate from older stocks. Plan transport to reduce delays and use packaging that protects produce without trapping excess moisture. Record losses to identify where improvements matter most.","Careful handling and suitable storage protect both food quality and the value of the harvest."],
["The Importance of Agricultural Research and Innovation","Agricultural Research","Discover how field trials, improved varieties, farmer knowledge and research partnerships contribute to agricultural progress.","Agricultural research investigates practical questions about crops, livestock, soils, water, pests, markets and farming systems. It helps test whether an idea works under particular conditions and identifies limitations before a practice is widely promoted.","Field trials compare treatments using a planned method and observations. Researchers may study varieties, planting dates, soil amendments or pest management approaches. Results from one location or season may not apply everywhere, so repeated testing and farmer participation are valuable.","Farmers can contribute by identifying local challenges, participating in trials and sharing observations. When reviewing research, check where and when it was conducted, which crop and variety were used, and whether the conditions resemble your own farm. Use recommendations from credible research and extension sources.","Good innovation combines scientific testing with local experience and practical access for farmers."],
["Understanding Livestock Integration in Crop Farming","Livestock and Farming","Explore how crops and livestock can support one another through manure, feed, residues and planned farm resource use.","Mixed crop-livestock farming links crop production with animals such as cattle, sheep, goats and poultry. The two activities can complement each other when feed, manure, labour, water and land are managed carefully.","Crop residues may provide feed, while manure can return nutrients and organic matter to fields after appropriate handling. Livestock can also provide income and other services. However, residues are also important for soil cover, and grazing pressure can damage land if it is not managed.","Plan how much residue is needed for soil protection and how much can safely be used as feed. Store and handle manure to reduce nutrient losses and contamination. Match herd size to available feed and water, and use grazing plans that allow vegetation to recover.","Integration works best when farm resources are balanced rather than overused."],
["How to Improve Crop Productivity Through Proper Farm Management","Crop Production","Bring together field preparation, planting calendars, crop monitoring, weed control and record keeping for more consistent production.","Crop productivity is influenced by many connected decisions throughout the season. Good management begins before planting and continues through crop establishment, growth, harvest and post-harvest handling. A single input cannot compensate for every other constraint.","Prepare fields according to soil condition and crop needs. Plant at an appropriate time and depth, use suitable spacing, and monitor emergence. Weed management is especially important while crops are young. Observe fields regularly so nutrient stress, pests, diseases or water problems can be addressed early.","Keep simple records of planting dates, varieties, field operations, input use, rainfall and harvest results. Compare seasons to identify what worked under similar conditions. Seek local recommendations before changing fertilizer or pesticide use.","Consistent observation and timely decisions often matter as much as the inputs used."],
["The Role of Bees and Pollinators in Agriculture","Sustainable Agriculture","Learn why bees and other pollinators matter for crop reproduction, biodiversity and resilient farming landscapes.","Pollination transfers pollen within or between flowers and enables seed or fruit development in many crops. Bees are important pollinators, alongside butterflies, flies, beetles, birds and other animals. The level of dependence varies by crop and variety.","Pollinators need food, nesting places and safe habitats throughout the year. Diverse flowering plants, hedgerows and natural vegetation can provide resources. Some pesticide uses can harm pollinators, especially when products are applied to flowering crops or when bees are actively foraging.","Avoid unnecessary pesticide applications, follow label directions and local rules, and use integrated pest management. Where possible, maintain flowering field margins and protect nesting habitats. Farmers should identify which pollinators visit their crops before choosing habitat measures.","Healthy pollinator communities support biodiversity and can contribute to better production in pollinator-dependent crops."],
["Agricultural Digital Platforms and Market Access","Agricultural Technology","See how digital platforms can connect farmers with buyers, prices, services and useful agricultural information.","Digital agricultural platforms use websites, mobile applications and communication services to connect people and information across the agricultural value chain. They may help farmers discover buyers, compare market information, access advisory services or arrange logistics.","A marketplace can make products more visible, but a digital listing alone does not guarantee a sale. Accurate quantities, quality descriptions, location, availability and clear communication help build trust. Price information should include date, market location and product grade to avoid misleading comparisons.","Check platform rules, fees, privacy practices and dispute procedures before using a service. Confirm buyer identity and payment arrangements, keep written records and avoid sharing unnecessary personal information. Combine online information with local market knowledge.","Digital platforms can widen access to information and buyers when listings are accurate and transactions are handled carefully."],
["The Future of Agriculture in Ethiopia","Agricultural Research","Consider how research, youth participation, technology, resilient production and stronger market links can shape Ethiopia’s agricultural future.","Agriculture remains central to livelihoods, food systems and rural development in Ethiopia. The sector is diverse, spanning different climates, landscapes, crops, livestock systems and market conditions. This diversity means progress depends on solutions that fit local realities.","Opportunities include improving access to quality seed and extension services, strengthening soil and water management, reducing post-harvest losses, supporting agricultural research and expanding responsible digital services. Young people can contribute through farming, entrepreneurship, research, engineering and information services.","Progress requires collaboration among farmers, cooperatives, researchers, public institutions, businesses and communities. Investment should consider affordability, skills, infrastructure, environmental protection and fair access. Local evidence and farmer feedback should guide the adoption of new approaches.","A productive agricultural future is built through inclusive innovation, practical knowledge and sustainable care for land and water."]
];

